    "case_summary": "A 25-year-old woman with a seven-year history of intravenous drug use was admitted to the hospital after experiencing fever and chills for six days. Her vital signs were as follows: temperature of 39°C, heart rate of 110 bpm, and blood pressure of 125/70 mmHg. The cardiac examination did not reveal any abnormalities, and the rest of the physical examination was also unremarkable. Laboratory results showed a white blood cell count of 12,000 cells/mm³, an erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 100 mm/hr, and a C-reactive protein level of 5 mg/100 ml (0.5 upper limit). Three sets of peripheral blood samples were collected from different sites.\n\nA structure due to an eustachian valve was noted in the right atrium. A mobile mass of 1.5 cm × 0.8 cm was noted to be attached to the eustachian valve. Another mobile mass of 0.6 cm × 0.8 cm was noted on tricuspid valve. After 5 days from admission in the hospital, 3 blood cultures grew methicillin susceptible staphylococcus aureus.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
